Under `--modular-termination`, a bounded callable can now be formed, applied and stored. `bounded.certify(a, f)` makes a `bounded<O, F>` whose function runs under the bound `a`: a lambda's body is checked under `a`, the function of a bounded value needs a bound at most `a`, and a named function needs a `decreases {:global}` bound at most `a`. `c.bound` (ghost) and `c.erase` project a value, and applying one, `c(x)`, `c.erase(x)`, `c.requires(x)` or `c.reads(x)`, needs `c.bound` strictly below the bound of the code that applies it. So an object can hold a call budget in a field and spend it later. A constructor may store into a field a bounded value that calls the object's members. `bounded<ORDINAL, F>` is now allowed wherever `bounded<nat, F>` is. Without the option, nothing changes. See https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/266.
